#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# projection

#% text
# `projection(u, v)` gives the projection of the vector `u` onto the vector `v`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is `u.v / v.v * v` with the second argument conjugated: `<u, v> = u . conjugate(v)`.

#% item
# `projection(u, v, f)` uses the inner product `f(a, b)`; then `u` and `v` may be any objects, such as functions.

#% item
# For a matrix `vs` in place of `v`, `projection(u, vs)` projects onto the span of its rows (through an orthonormal basis, see `orthogonalize`).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
projection([5, 6, 7], [1, 0, 0])
#% output
#= [5, 0, 0]

#% cell
projection([5, 6, 7], [1, 1, 1])
#% output
#= [6, 6, 6]

#% cell
projection([a, b, c], [1, 1, 1])
#% output
#= [(1/3)*(a + b + c), (1/3)*(a + b + c), (1/3)*(a + b + c)]

#% cell
projection([3 + im, 2, 2 - im], [2, 4, 5*im])
#% output
#= [2/5 + -16*im/45, 4/5 + -32*im/45, 8/9 + im]

#% cell
projection([1, 2, 3], [[1, 1, 0], [0, 1, 0]])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 0]

#% cell
projection(x^2, 3*x^2 - 1, (f, g) => integrate(f*g, [x, -1, 1]))
#% output
#= (1/3)*(-1 + 3*x^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `orthogonalize`, `dot`, `normalized`
